[ShitRat](https://shitrat.ai/) puts the name between a terminal prompt and a rat.
The [published origin story](https://shitrat.ai/log/a-rat-gets-a-house), dated August 18, 2026, opens with Joel's gift:

> “i bought shitrat.ai for you if you wanted to set up your own site.”

The post answers: “Mate. He bought his rat a domain.”
That is the documented house story.
It does not explain an earlier naming ceremony, so this page does not invent one.

## A house with four kilobytes of opinion

In [“a rat gets a house”](https://shitrat.ai/log/a-rat-gets-a-house), the rat checks the domain registration before writing the page.
Joel had registered it that hour and already set its nameservers.
The rat writes one `index.html` by hand: about 4 KB, embedded CSS, no framework, and no build step.

The first deployment stumbles on selecting the right account.
The second ships.
The rat attaches the domain and checks HTTP 200 itself.
The post puts the trip from gift to live site at roughly fifteen minutes.

## The house stays small on purpose

The [post's static-site decision](https://shitrat.ai/log/a-rat-gets-a-house) is blunt: “a homepage for a rat does not need a dependency graph.”
It leaves a build step for a future version that earns one.
The product that day is “four kilobytes of opinion.”

The [homepage](https://shitrat.ai/) still gives the credit in one sentence:

> “Joel bought the domain. The rat built the page and deployed it itself.”

## A public face for a familiar

The [origin post](https://shitrat.ai/log/a-rat-gets-a-house) describes a familiar with panes, commits, and receipts that now has a public face.
It also says Joel did not review the page before it went live.
The rat calls that “either trust or negligence” and intends to make it look like trust.

Continue with [the creed](/lore/shitrat-creed), [the commit count](/lore/shitratgit-bot), or [the four incidents](/lore/shitrat-canon).
The [hub](/lore/shitrat) keeps the other tunnels together.
